86045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523850 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523850 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523850 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7c700890-f7ec-5058-9334-5a60b70722d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523917 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa8e2a76-bef8-546c-bf0c-5ea711bff307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523970 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77bfba8d-0add-517b-b669-b916e6dff897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523970 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{9}{(1)^{4}}=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-308821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523994 #edb/ref 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{3}$ at the point $(1,1)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a153fbfd-b7f1-5770-9660-ea9e18ac215d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523994 #edb/ref 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{2}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-(1)) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,4,0\\,).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524012 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}$ at the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccfa4d84-7836-5556-9446-f447928d0c91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524012 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524021 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-7x$ at the point $(-1,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e108591f-1dbb-58f4-aca2-6f29ab0389ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524021 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-7x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-7x)=6{x}^{2}-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(-1)=6(-1)^{2}-7=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-1)}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
